WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:09.100
The Iowa Caucuses typically force
candidates to lay out their farm program

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:13.389
platforms. Both president Trump and his
Democratic challengers are promising to

00:00:13.389 --> 00:00:18.760
continue or expand programs that provide
large subsidies to farmers. The one clear

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:22.060
difference between the president and
most of his challengers is that many

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:26.830
Democrats would direct more subsidies to
smaller farms. The president also gave

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:31.060
farmers $25 billion in
ad-hoc market facilitation payments to

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:35.440
compensate them for the damage his trade
policies have done to their revenues.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.800
Not that there's much difference between
Mr. Trump and most of the challengers on trade.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.960
Like the president, they seem to
believe that US exports are good, but US

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.340
imports are bad, so trade deals that
lower tariffs are bad. Only Michael

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:52.629
Bloomberg supports free trade. Finally,
most candidates are paying little

00:00:52.629 --> 00:00:56.320
attention to public funding for
agricultural research, one program that

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:01.620
benefits farmers and consumers and keeps
US agriculture globally competitive.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:06.160
Instead, they prefer to spend taxpayer
dollars on direct deposit subsidies to

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:09.000
farmers to win their rural votes.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.000
What do you think about the presidential
candidates' farm programs?

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.670
Let us know in our poll. Also, let us know what other

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:20.500
topics you'd like our scholars to cover
in 60 seconds and be sure to LIKE and

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:26.369
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

